              text: 'Explain your thinking in each case. (a) + + (b) – – (c) + + (d) + + (e) – – 2.2 Reading and Evaluating Complex Expressions Sometimes, when an expression is not accompanied by a context, there can be more than one way of evaluating its value. In such cases, we need some tools and rules to specify how exactly the expression has to be evaluated. To give an example with language, look at the following sentences: (a) Sentence: “Shalini sat next to a friend with toys”. Meaning: The friend has toys and Shalini sat next to her. (b) Sentence: “Shalini sat next to a friend, with toys”. Meaning: Shalini has the toys and she sat with them next to her friend. This sentence without the punctuation could have been interpreted in two different ways. The appropriate use of a comma specifies how the sentence has to be understood. Let us see an expression that can be evaluated in more than one way. Example 4: Mallesh brought marbles to the playground. Arun brought bags of marbles with marbles in each bag. How many marbles did Mallesh and Arun bring to the playground? Mallesh summarized this by writing the mathematical expression — + × 4. -2.indd -2.indd Without knowing the context behind this expression, Purna found the value of this expression to be 140. He added and first, to get 35, and then multiplied by to get 140. Mallesh found the value of this expression to be 50. He multiplied and first to get and added to to get 50. In this case, Mallesh is right. But why did Purna get it wrong?',
